1	# Lisply Backend Requirements
2	
3	This document outlines the requirements for any Lisp-based program to
4	become a "Lisply-compliant" backend for use with the MCP (Model
5	Context Protocol) wrapper implemented by this repository. Compliant
6	Lisply backend Lisp environments enable direct AI-assisted symbolic
7	programming, through protocols defined in this document.
8	
9	## Core Requirements
10	
11	### 2. Some kind of Lisp
12	
13	It should be a [Lisp](https://common-lisp.net/). What is a Lisp? Well
14	for our purposes let's say a Lisp is a program which can accept
15	_expressions_ in some known syntax, and can _evaluate_ the expression
16	to produce possibly some output and probably one or more _return
17	values_. So, strictly speaking, according to our rather loose
18	definition, we're really talking more about a
19	[REPL](https://common-lisp.net/project/slime/) than a
20	[Lisp](https://www.paulgraham.com/lisp.html) per se. But you'll have a
21	better time if the engine behind your
22	[REPL](https://lisp-lang.org/learn/repl) is indeed a
23	[Lisp](https://franz.com/products/allegro-common-lisp/).
24	
25	### 1. HTTP Server Capability
26	
27	A Lisply backend must provide an HTTP server that exposes the
28	following endpoints:
29	
30	- `/lisply/ping-lisp`: Simple ping endpoint for availability checks
31	- `/lisply/lisp-eval`: Endpoint for evaluating Lisp expressions
32	- `/lisply/tools/list`: Endpoint that returns a list of available tools
33	
34	Note: The endpoint prefix (`/lisply/`) can be configured in the MCP
35	wrapper, but backends should support this as the default.
36	
37	### 2. Lisp Evaluation Protocol
38	
39	The backend must support Lisp code evaluation through the
40	`/lisply/lisp-eval` endpoint with the following characteristics:
41	
42	- **Request Format**: HTTP POST accepting JSON payload with:
43	  ```json
44	  {
45	    "code": "Lisp code to evaluate",
46	    "package": "Optional package name"
47	  }
48	  ```
49	
50	- **Response Format**: JSON response with the following structure:
51	  ```json
52	  {
53	    "success": true,
54	    "result": "Result of evaluation",
55	    "stdout": "Any stdout output generated"
56	  }
57	  ```
58	  
59	  Or in case of error:
60	  ```json
61	  {
62	    "success": false,
63	    "error": "Error message"
64	  }
65	  ```
66	
67	### 3. Tool Definitions
68	
69	The backend must expose a list of its capabilities through the
70	`/lisply/tools/list` endpoint, which returns a JSON object with the
71	structure:
72	
73	```json
74	{
75	  "tools": [
76	    {
77	      "name": "lisp_eval",
78	      "description": "Evaluates Lisp code directly within the Lisply environment",
79	      "inputSchema": {
80	        "type": "object",
81	        "properties": {
82	          "code": {
83	            "type": "string",
84	            "description": "The Lisp code to evaluate"
85	          },
86	          "package": {
87	            "type": "string",
88	            "description": "The package to use for the evaluation (optional)"
89	          }
90	        },
91	        "required": ["code"]
92	      }
93	    },
94	    {
95	      "name": "ping_lisp",
96	      "description": "Checks if the Lisply backend is available",
97	      "inputSchema": {
98	        "type": "object",
99	        "properties": {}
100	      }
101	    }
102	  ]
103	}
104	```
105	
106	**Note**: The backend is only expected to implement the `lisp_eval`
107	and `ping_lisp` tools. The `http_request` tool and the `mode`
108	parameter for `lisp_eval` are handled by the MCP wrapper middleware
109	(mcp-wrapper.js) and should not be implemented or documented by the
110	backend. The backend is not aware of these features, as they are an
111	abstraction provided by the middleware.
112	
113	## Optional Capabilities
114	
115	### 1. SWANK Server Support
116	
117	For enhanced integration with development environments:
118	
119	- **SWANK Protocol**: Support for connecting via SWANK (Superior Lisp
120	  Interaction Mode for Emacs) for Lisply backends which support that
121	  (e.g. Common Lisp based backends)
122	- **Default Port**: 4200 (internal to container) / 4201 (visible on
123	  docker host)
124	
125	### 2. Interactive Debugger
126	
127	For local deployments, provide interactive debugging capabilities:
128	
129	- Interactive REPL via stdio mode
130	- Debugger interface with the ability to:
131	  - Display debug information for errors
132	  - Present multiple restart options
133	  - Support for common debugging commands (abort, continue, backtrace, etc.)
134	  - Return to normal evaluation upon command
135	- Proper handling of debugger prompt detection (implementation-specific)
136	
137	### 3. Communication Modes
138	
139	The middleware supports two evaluation modes with the Lisply
140	backend:
141	
142	- **HTTP Mode**: Structured communication with separate result and
143	  stdout fields. The backend returns a JSON response with success,
144	  result, and stdout fields. This is the standard mode of
145	  communication and the only one the backend needs to implement
146	  directly.
147	
148	- **Stdio Mode**: To support this (optional) mode, the backend must
149	  present a Lisp REPL (read-eval-print-loop) as the foreground process
150	  attached to its standard input/output. The communication for this
151	  mode is managed by the middleware (mcp-wrapper.js), which expects a
152	  native REPL interface on the standard input/output streams of the
153	  local backend container. Implementing/configuring this standard I/O
154	  behavior may be easier on some backends (e.g. Common Lisp, where a
155	  repl on standard I/O is typically the default anyway, vs. Emacs
156	  Lisp, where some tricks may be necessary to get something like
157	  [IELM](https://www.emacswiki.org/emacs/InferiorEmacsLispMode) to
158	  show up on standard I/O.
159	
160	
161	### 4. HTTPS Support
162	
163	For secure deployments:
164	
165	- HTTPS server with valid certificates
166	- Default port: 9443 (internal to container) / 9444 (visible on docker host)
167	
168	### 5. Telnet Interface
169	
170	For legacy access methods:
171	
172	- Telnet server for direct Lisp interaction
173	- Default port: 4023 (internal to container) / 4024 (visible on docker host)
174	
175	## Containerization Support (optional in principle, but required for local operation of lisply-mcp)
176	
177	For standardized deployment, backends should support:
178	
179	1. **Docker Container**: A Docker image containing the Lisply backend
180	2. **Service Configuration**: Environment variables to configure service startup:
181	   - START_HTTP, HTTP_PORT
182	   - START_HTTPS, HTTPS_PORT
183	   - START_SWANK, SWANK_PORT
184	   - START_TELNET, TELNET_PORT
185	
186	3. **Volume Mounting**: Support for mounting host directories into the container
187	
188	## Implementation Examples
189	
190	Currently, there are implementations or planned implementations for:
191	
192	1. **Gendl**: A full implementation available at [Gendl on GitLab](https://gitlab.common-lisp.net/gendl/gendl)
193	        with Lisply implementation [here](https://gitlab.common-lisp.net/gendl/gendl/gwl/lisply-backend)
194	2. **GNU Emacs Backend**: In development at [Skewed Emacs on GitHub](https://github.com/gornskew/skewed-emacs.git)
195	
196	
197	## Testing for Compliance
198	
199	To test a backend for compliance, implement the following checks:
200	
201	1. Ping test: `GET /lisply/ping-lisp`
202	2. Tool list retrieval: `GET /lisply/tools/list`
203	3. Basic Lisp evaluation: 
204	   ```
205	   POST /lisply/lisp-eval
206	   {"code": "(+ 1 2 3)"}
207	   ```
208	4. Package specification (for backends that support package-based namespaces):
209	   ```
210	   POST /lisply/lisp-eval
211	   {"code": "(package-name *package*)", "package": "gdl-user"}
212	   ```
213	
214	Note: Testing of the `mode` parameter should be done at the middleware
215	level, not directly with the backend, as this feature is handled by
216	the MCP wrapper and is expected to be ignored by the backend.
217	
218	A successful implementation should respond correctly to all these tests.
219	
